Excellent — this is the crux of the design. The key hook point is crystal clear:

```rust
// activities.rs:194 — the single place posture must be applied
fn run_context(&self, cancel: CancellationToken) -> RunContext<Ctx> {
    RunContext::ephemeral((self.ctx_factory)()).with_cancel(cancel)
}
```

And core's `RunContext` already has every builder method I need (`with_permission_mode`, `with_permission_policy`, `with_deny_rules`, `with_allow_rules`, `with_approval_handler`, `with_guard_rules`, `without_default_guards`, `without_output_redaction`, `with_extra_secrets`). Critically, `permission_policy` and `approval_handler` are `Arc<dyn Trait>` — **non-serializable**, so they must live worker-side.

Let me read the remaining decision-critical files: `permission.rs` (are DenyRule/AllowRule/GuardRule serializable?), the client boundary (`runner.rs`), the activity-threading (`workflow.rs`), and the spec §5.8 as-built note.

